ghpull:796 a bl:PullRequest, bl:WorkItem ;
    rdfs:label "datalog: skip rule-match probes for QuadWildcard shapes no rule body uses" ;
    bl:number 796 ;
    bl:state bl:Open ;
    bl:closesIssue ghissues:777 .

session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr796 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:777 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-11-14T16:20:53Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-11-14T16:33:43Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr796Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #777 claimed GO materialisation scans 83k rules per iteration and asked for a rule-count-based skip test (100 vs 10,000 rules). Reading reasoner.rs/datalog.rs first showed B8-B10 (prior PERFORMANCE.md work) already made fact-side lookup O(1) per quad via rule_map hashing, independent of rule count — so the literal rule-count test would likely already pass on main, and wasn't the real bottleneck. The actual cost (per B10's own measurement: 7.2s for 1.4M quads, 0 inferences) is a constant-factor one: every delta quad unconditionally builds and hashes all 16 QuadWildcard generalisations even though GO's rules use only one shape. Fix: track which of the 16 shapes rule_map/neg_rule_map actually contain (a u16 bitmask, updated by new() and add_rule()) and only generate+probe those shapes per quad. This targets quad-count scaling with narrow shapes, not rule-count scaling per se — made explicit in both the PR body and PERFORMANCE.md's B11 entry rather than overstating what the fix measures." ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr796Decision ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:796 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr796 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr796Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Chose a shape-bitmask pre-filter (quad_wildcard_shape/wildcard_shape_bit/wildcard_quad_pattern_for_shapes) that restricts get_rules_for_fact's 16-combination generation down to only the shapes some rule body atom actually uses, maintained incrementally in DatalogProgram::new/add_rule and verified by deterministic unit tests (shape mask stays single-bit at 100 vs 10,000 rules; mixed narrow+wide-shaped rules both still fire; add_rule updates the mask)." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered writing only the wall-clock 100-vs-10,000-rule test the issue literally asked for and treating a pass as 'done'. Rejected after tracing the actual cost model: with a fixed delta, that test's ratio is dominated by DatalogProgram::new's unavoidable O(rules) indexing cost, not by the per-quad probe count the issue's own GO measurement (7.2s for 1.4M quads) was actually bottlenecked on — a wall-clock-only test would have given a false sense of having fixed the real problem. Kept the wall-clock test anyway (marked #[ignore], as a sanity check matching the issue's literal wording) but added the deterministic shape-mask tests as the real regression guard, and said so plainly in the PR body instead of overclaiming." .
